// DEEP-TIME CHAIN — the mean*AtAge family
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Doc 99's canonical chain: t_Ma → LOD(t) by angular-momentum conservation on
// the cited Moon-distance history (Farhat et al. 2022, plan 12 option A) → the
// composed lunisolar precession clock T_p(t) → AU(t), year_s(t), the Moon's
// distance and months, the anomalistic year, the stellar/sidereal days, the
// planets' semi-major axes and the Earth–planet distances. Every function
// returns the modern constant exactly at t_Ma = 0. ONE home since plan 13 B2:
// @essrt/physics deltat/deep-time-chain (_dtc below); the functions here are
// its readers under the names the panels and charts call.
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ───── Earth physical constants ─────
// EARTH_MOI_FACTOR (α at J2000) lives in section C3 "Deep-time physics anchors" near the top.
const R_EARTH_M        = (diameters.earthDiameter / 2) * 1000;        // km → m
const I_EARTH          = EARTH_MOI_FACTOR * M_EARTH_ALONE * R_EARTH_M * R_EARTH_M;
                       // ≈ 8.034 × 10³⁷ kg·m² — anchor value of Earth's polar moment at J2000.
                       // Used for the L_TOTAL_EM angular-momentum anchor below; the time-
                       // varying value for LOD evolution is iEarthAtAge(t_Ma).

// ─── α(t): L1-orbital-coupled GIA contribution to polar moment ───
//
// Earth's polar moment coefficient α = C/(M·R²) is NOT strictly constant. As
// continents rebound from the last ice age — and, on 100-kyr timescales, as
// ice sheets grow and retreat under Milankovitch orbital forcing — crustal
// mass moves toward or away from the rotation axis, changing C and hence α.
// The modern rate is measured from satellite gravimetry (LAGEOS SLR since
// 1979, GRACE since 2002):
//
//     dJ₂/dt ≈ −2.7 × 10⁻¹¹ /yr   (Cox & Chao 2002, JGR; confirmed by
//                                  Cheng, Tapley & Ries 2013, JGR)
//
// Translating dJ₂/dt to dα/dt requires the geometry of mass redistribution.
// For axisymmetric mass moving equator → pole (the GIA case):
//     ΔC per unit mass = −R²;  ΔA per unit mass = +R²/2
//     ⇒ ΔJ₂ = −1.5·m/M  while  Δα = ΔC/(M·R²) = −m/M
//     ⇒ dα/dt = dJ₂/dt / 1.5 = −1.8 × 10⁻¹¹ /yr at J2000
//
// The framework's implementation couples α(t) directly to the same L1
// orbital layer that drives the Climate Formula (itself fit against LR04
// δ¹⁸O):
//
//     α(t) = α_J2000 − k · [ L1(t) − ⟨L1⟩_τ(t) ]
//
// One ice history, one response function: the L1 layer is the ice-volume
// proxy, and the solid Earth answers it through the mantle's viscoelastic
// relaxation (⟨L1⟩_τ = the causal exponential average over the past).
// An uncompensated ice load lowers α (k > 0); after a rapid deglaciation
// α sits ABOVE equilibrium and relaxes for ~τ — today's −0.35 ms/cy is
// that tail. Home: @essrt/physics/climate/l1-orbital createAlphaGiaChannel
// (plan 06 D7 — measured against the historical ΔT record).
//
// Named physical constants — no fitting to eclipse data:
//   • EARTH_MOI_FACTOR            — IERS Conventions 2010 anchor at J2000
//   • ALPHA_GIA_RATE_J2000_PER_YR — Cox & Chao 2002 ÷ Peltier factor; k is DERIVED from it at runtime
//   • ALPHA_GIA_RELAXATION_KYR    — τ, measured by the ΔT joint fitter (optimum 5–6 kyr; Maxwell degree-2 estimate 4–6 kyr)
//
// CRITICAL: α(t) is purely an Earth-INTERNAL mass redistribution. It does
// NOT transfer angular momentum to the Moon, so the Moon distance evolution
// (Farhat 2022) and Kepler's 3rd law for the Moon are UNAFFECTED. Only the
// L_Earth ↔ ω partition shifts at each epoch; L_Total_EM remains conserved.
//
// Historical note: an earlier implementation used a multi-mode viscoelastic
// GIA form (Peltier 2004 ICE-5G(VM2), 3 exponential modes with τ derived
// from Maxwell rheology × spherical-harmonic geometric factors). That form
// produced a slope discontinuity at t_Ma = 0 (LOD chart kink at J2000); the
// L1-orbital-coupled form here is smooth everywhere (C∞) and reproduces the
// Milankovitch α oscillation as an emergent prediction. See doc 99 §
// "Refinement: climate-driven α(t)" for the transition rationale.

/** α(t) — polar moment coefficient at age t_Ma (millions of years before J2000).
 *
 *  Option 4-climate model (2026-07-01): couples α to the same L1 orbital
 *  layer that drives the climate formula. The physical chain is:
 *      Planetary eigenmodes → Milankovitch orbital forcing →
 *      Ice-mass redistribution → GIA J₂/α → LOD
 *  Each link is documented literature: Hays/Imbrie/Shackleton 1976 (orbital
 *  → ice), Peltier ICE-5G/6G_C (ice → GIA), Chao 2016 (α → LOD). Cheng
 *  et al. 2011 LAGEOS J₂ non-linearity partially confirms glacial-cycle
 *  signature in J₂ observations.
 *
 *  Replaces the previous Math.abs(t_Ma) symmetric GIA relaxation, which
 *  produced a slope discontinuity at J2000 (LOD chart kink). The climate-
 *  driven form is smooth everywhere (C∞), preserves α(J2000) = EARTH_MOI_
 *  FACTOR exactly, and reproduces the glacial-cycle α oscillation as an
 *  emergent prediction from the same orbital physics fitted against δ¹⁸O.
 *
 *  Modern anchor: dα/dt at J2000 = -1.35e-11 /yr, derived from Cox & Chao 2002
 *  dJ₂/dt = -2.7e-11 /yr via J₂→α conversion factor 2.0 (Peltier ICE-6G LOD-
 *  coupling range; was factor 1.5 per Cheng-Tapley-Ries 2013). The choice of
 *  factor 2.0 was pinned by empirical L-5b|R| optimum with LLR-anchored α₁ —
 *  physically defensible given the model-dependent J₂→α conversion uncertainty.
 *
 *  Sign: warmer (lower δ¹⁸O = interglacial) ↔ less continental ice ↔
 *  mass shifts equatorward → smaller α. Peltier & Wu 1984.
 *
 *  Regime: uses lr04-post-mpt L1 coefficients throughout. The formula is
 *  a periodic sum over the engine's own orbital lines (periods in kyr,
 *  data/l1-physical-lines.json), so it stays bounded at deep time.
 *  Beyond ±1 Myr the extrapolation is a smooth continuation of the fitted
 *  periodic pattern, not a physics prediction. */
// (the GIA channel's regime, 'lr04-post-mpt', is named inside the deep-time chain — B2)
// Calibration: dα/dt at J2000 = −1.35e-11/yr — Cox & Chao 2002 dJ₂/dt =
// −2.7e-11/yr ÷ the conversion factor 2.0 (Peltier ICE-6G LOD-coupling range).
// The GIA channel — the LAGGED response of the polar moment to the L1 ice
// history: ONE home in @essrt/physics/climate/l1-orbital (createAlphaGiaChannel
// — k derived from the Cox–Chao rate, τ measured against the historical ΔT
// record); the deep-time chain builds it.
// ───── THE deep-time chain — ONE home (plan 13 B2) ─────
// @essrt/physics deltat/deep-time-chain assembles, from the generated
// constants, the fitted coefficients, the deep-mode table and the cited
// Moon-distance history, everything the browser used to assemble here in six
// lazy factories: the GIA α channel with its memo and its α pin, the Layer-0
// bundle, the recession history, the ΔT cycle stack, |s₃|, the LOD/ΔT core,
// the ONE deep e and the lunar month chain. tools/lib/deep-time.js and the
// package model destructure the same chain. The browser's factory getters
// below (_alphaGia, _recessionHistory, _deepLod, _dtCycles, _deepEcc,
// _moonChain) keep their names as delegates for their ~60 readers; the
// research toggles reach the chain through flagsAt (a cycle's ΔT and δLOD
// terms switch together here — cycleLodFollowsFlags). Lazy: the flags and
// FIT are read at first use, after module evaluation.
let _dtcM = null;
function _dtc() {
  if (_dtcM === null) {
    _dtcM = createDeepTimeChain({
      constants: K, fitted: FIT, deepModes: DEEP_MODES_ARTIFACT, moonDistanceArtifact: MOON_DISTANCE_ARTIFACT,
      flagsAt: () => ({
        bond: BOND_DT_CORRECTION_ENABLED, hallstatt: HALLSTATT_DT_CORRECTION_ENABLED,
        jose5: JOSE5_DT_CORRECTION_ENABLED, jose4: JOSE4_DT_CORRECTION_ENABLED,
        resonator: RESONATOR_DT_CORRECTION_ENABLED, cycleLodFollowsFlags: true,
      }),
    });
  }
  return _dtcM;
}
/** The GIA channel (createAlphaGiaChannel on the 'lr04-post-mpt' regime) — the chain's. */
function _alphaGia() { return _dtc().alphaGia; }

// R2 — the α reference pin: while the deep calendar's cumulative-days table is
// BUILT, α is held at its J2000 reference, or the table defines itself in
// terms of its own output (α(t)'s MEAN over the build window sits ~+1.24e-6
// above the IERS anchor — an interglacial peak). Save/restore under
// try/finally, so a throw mid-build cannot leave every α consumer pinned.
// The pin, the exact-argument memo (35 % of deep-time frame cost in α(t)
// before it) and the channel read are the chain's (B2).
function _withLatticeAlpha(build) { return _dtc().withLatticeAlpha(build); }
function earthMoiFactorAtAge(t_Ma) { return _dtc().earthMoiFactorAtAge(t_Ma); }

// ─── Mantle-core electromagnetic coupling: NOT included as a constant LOD term ───
//
// Mantle-core coupling is a real physical channel (EM + topographic torques at the
// CMB; decadal LOD oscillations of ±1-2 ms; secular trend estimated at -0.1 to
// -0.3 ms/century from Holme 1998 / Mound & Buffett 2003). It WAS implemented
// here as a constant LOD secular offset, but the L-5b/L-7 cross-validation
// definitively showed that the modern Holme value, when extrapolated linearly
// over 2720 years into the past, OVER-CORRECTS the BCE-era ΔT by ~2700 s.
//
// This is itself a scientific finding: the historical eclipse record proves
// the mantle-core secular rate has NOT been constant over millennia. A proper
// time-varying model is required (cf. Stephenson & Morrison 2004 piecewise
// polynomial fit, which exists precisely because of this) but is out of scope
// for a first-principles literature-constant treatment. We therefore omit the
// term entirely rather than introduce a phenomenological time profile.
//
// See doc 102 § "Mantle-core coupling: a positive null result" for details.

/** I_Earth(t) = α(t) · M · R² */
function iEarthAtAge(t_Ma) {
  return earthMoiFactorAtAge(t_Ma) * M_EARTH_ALONE * R_EARTH_M * R_EARTH_M;
}

// ───── Solar physics constants (Driver 2 — mass loss) ─────
// L_SUN_W and SOLAR_WIND_KG_PER_S live in section C3 "Deep-time physics anchors" near the top.
const C_SI_M_PER_S         = speedOfLight * 1000;   // km/s → m/s (exact IAU c)
const dM_dt_radiation_kg_s = L_SUN_W / (C_SI_M_PER_S * C_SI_M_PER_S);  // ≈ 4.26 × 10⁹
const dM_dt_total_kg_s     = dM_dt_radiation_kg_s + SOLAR_WIND_KG_PER_S;  // ≈ 5.86 × 10⁹
const SOLAR_MASS_LOSS_FRAC_PER_YR =
      dM_dt_total_kg_s * meansiderealyearlengthinSeconds / M_SUN;     // ≈ 9.30 × 10⁻¹⁴ /yr

// ───── LOD anchor: the J2000 day — the IAU sidereal year in seconds over the
// IAU sidereal year in days = the SI day (the identifier is historical) ─────
const LOD_NOW_H13_S = meansiderealyearlengthinSeconds / meansiderealyearlengthinDays_kinematic;

// ───── J2000 anchor snapshots (frozen at module load) ─────
// Captured BEFORE Phase 1 converts the script.js globals
// meanlengthofday, meansolaryearlengthinDays,
// meansiderealyearlengthinDays, and meansiderealyearlengthinSeconds from
// `const` to `let`. The mean*AtAge() functions in this block reference
